    "lawCode": "Amd §5-322.1, Gen Ob L",
    "lawSection": "General Obligations Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S800\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the general obligations law, in relation to a\nsubcontractor's or materialman's right to file a claim and/or commence\nan action upon a payment bond\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo forbid agreements in construction contracts to condition\nsubcontractor's and materialman's rights to file a claim and/or\ncommence an action on a payment bond on exhaustion of another legal\nremedy.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 5-322.1 of the General Obligations Law is amended to declare\nvoid and unenforceable a construction contract provision conditioning\na subcontractor's and materialman's right to file claim and/or\ncommence an action on payment bond on exhaustion of another legal\nremedy.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nSince the New York State Court of Appeals West-Fair case which placed\nthe risk of non payment by the owner on the contractor, contractors\nand their attorneys have been attempting to circumvent that decision\nand place the risk for non payment on the subcontractor. This\n\"conditional provision\" in effect denies the subcontractor and\nmaterialman its right to timely file and commence an action on the\npayment bond. Waiting until such time, as the subcontractor or\nmaterialman has exhausted its lien remedies or other legal remedies,\nwill in all likelihood cause the one year statute of limitations for\ncommencing an action on the bond to expire, thus resulting in shifting\nthe risk of non payment from the bonding company and the contractor to\nthe subcontractor and materialman. The intent of such a conditional\nprovision is to interfere with the subcontractor and materialman's 3rd\nparty beneficiary rights under the payment bond. Clearly such a\nprovision is against public policy and should be declared void and\nunenforceable.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008:  A.9638 Passed Assembly\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
